findings:
- The client constructs RouteOpenSupervisor only as an unused `_route_owner`; product resolution/proxy dispatch still uses one global resolve request, path attempt, proxy request, selected connection, and terminal state.
- `--test-proxy-concurrency` is validated but is not consumed by the product proxy state machine. The concurrent-opens case counts 64 sequential opens from one process and therefore does not prove 64 simultaneous independently correlated opens.
- The new ConnectionManager relay singleflight, DCUtR generation, waiter, and surplus-close APIs are covered by state tests but are not dispatched by client main.
- The same-process p2x-net resolve/proxy tests still fabricate Resolved and Authorized responses instead of running the exchange resolution and server ticket owners required by Plan 05a section 5.3.
decision:
- The previous completed status is not accepted. Correct locally automatable implementation/evidence gaps before re-running the phase gate.
blockers: none

phase review remediation 1: product multi-open ownership
- Integrated RouteOpenSupervisor into the real client swarm path for finite multi-open execution instead of constructing it as an unused value.
- Added independently correlated resolve wire IDs, proxy request IDs, path attempts, handshake workers, absolute deadlines, terminal release, and a bounded owner window.
- Wired same-selector FIFO promotion, one exact resolve retransmission, path timeout processing, connection event fan-out, direct/relay exact-open dispatch, and worker result correlation.
- ConnectionManager now stores validated relay/capability/revision/expiry metadata, coordinates one relay dial for joined waiters, owns DCUtR generations, tracks waiter IDs, and dispatches surplus close actions after release.
- Strengthened concurrent-opens from 64 sequential results to 128 total opens through a real 64-open owner window; the runner requires an observed peak of exactly 64 and all 128 unique Resolve, path, ticket, and stream correlations.
validation:
- cargo test -p p2x-client --all-targets --all-features: passed (25 tests).
- cargo clippy -p p2x-client --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings: passed.
- tests/resolution/local.sh --case connection-reuse: passed with real product binaries.
- tests/resolution/local.sh --case concurrent-opens: passed with 128 total opens and a 64-open owner window.

phase review remediation 2: atomic server admission and owner pipeline
- Moved stream-ID allocation into TicketAdmissionLedger so live recheck, replay consume, and stream allocation form one owner transition; randomness failure leaves replay capacity untouched.
- Exposed the server admission modules through the package library and added a same-process integration test that sends a ticket issued by the actual exchange Resolver/Registry through immutable verification, live server admission, stream allocation, and replay rejection.
validation:
- cargo test -p p2x-server --all-targets --all-features: passed, including owner_pipeline.
- server Clippy initially identified the public len/is_empty API requirement after library exposure; is_empty was added and final Clippy will be rerun before commit.
blockers: none

phase review remediation 3: deadline, retention, retry, and executable gate closure
- Routed ordinary finite product opens through RouteOpenSupervisor, while retaining the focused legacy path only for explicit replay, mutation, delayed-response, direct-open fault, and restart-recovery diagnostics.
- Enforced the single absolute route deadline when a resolve response arrives and added regression coverage proving the resolver owner is released at the boundary.
- Retained exchange idempotency responses through the maximum supported 30-second ticket clock skew and added exact-boundary coverage.
- Classified explicit proxy capacity rejection as terminal instead of ambiguous. Fresh-ticket retries now occur only for the bounded recoverable set and release the prior connection-manager waiter/setup ownership before retrying.
- Made resolve-limit process evidence independent of client scheduling order: exactly one terminal must authorize and exactly one must reject with limit.resolve_requests, with exchange-side admission/ticket-state evidence still required.
- Updated protocol, client-connection, and resolution-runner documentation to match executable owner, concurrency, retention, and atomic admission behavior.
